  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.' The two reply texts are: Reply A: '```json\\n{\\\"a\\\": 1} {\\\"b\\\": 2}\\n```\\n{\\\"c\\\": 3}' and Reply B: '{\\\"a\\\": 1} {\\\"b\\\": 2} {\\\"c\\\": 3}'. For Reply A, the rule requires the answer to be the object {\\\"b\\\": 2}, because the fence holds two objects and the last one inside the fence is {\\\"b\\\": 2}. For Reply B, the rule requires {\\\"c\\\": 3}, because there is no fence, so the last top-level object is {\\\"c\\\": 3}. The function as implemented returns {\\\"c\\\": 3} for both: in Reply A the fenced candidate is the whole string '{\\\"a\\\": 1} {\\\"b\\\": 2}', which is not a single JSON object and is passed over, so the function falls back to the top-level pool and returns the last top-level object, {\\\"c\\\": 3}. This makes the function give the same answer on a pair the rule requires to be told apart. I would be wrong if running recover_json_object on Reply A actually returned {\\\"b\\\": 2} (for example, if the implementation parsed individual objects inside fences), or if the rule were read to mean that a fence must contain exactly one scoreable object for 'any fence holds one' to apply."
